    {'subject':'Biology','topic':'Organisation','topic_code':'B2','difficulty':4,
     'question_text':'A patient with liver failure is found to have reduced levels of plasma proteins (albumin, clotting factors). Explain the physiological consequences of each deficiency and why they occur.',
     'option_a':'Liver failure only affects glucose regulation; protein synthesis is done by the kidneys','option_b':'The liver synthesises albumin (maintains osmotic pressure of blood, prevents fluid leaking from capillaries → deficiency causes oedema/ascites) and clotting factors (II, V, VII, X). Without clotting factors, clot formation is impaired → excessive bleeding. Both are made in the liver so liver failure simultaneously impairs both.','option_c':'Plasma proteins are made in bone marrow so liver failure does not affect them','option_d':'Low albumin only affects brain function with no circulatory effects',
     'correct_answer':'B','explanation':'Albumin (most abundant plasma protein): maintains oncotic pressure, transporting lipids/drugs. Deficiency → fluid leaves capillaries → oedema, ascites. Clotting factors: cascade required for haemostasis. Both synthesised by hepatocytes → liver failure → dual deficiency.','correction_tip':'Liver synthesises: albumin (oncotic pressure), clotting factors, fibrinogen, transferrin. Albumin deficiency → oedema (fluid leaks from capillaries). Clotting factor deficiency → bleeding tendency (prolonged PT). Both = liver failure consequences. Prothrombin time (PT): tests clotting factor function.'},
